{
  "pages": [
    {
      "page_idx": 11,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.153,
            0.05,
            0.432,
            0.066
          ],
          "content": "Ganita Prakash | Grade 7 | Part-II"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.089,
            0.846,
            0.128
          ],
          "content": "Choosing more than five occurrences of 2s will give a common multiple; but it will not be the lowest. Are you able to see why?"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.137,
            0.534,
            0.159
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "How many 3s should the LCM contain?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.163,
            0.847,
            0.221
          ],
          "content": "The factorisation of 96 contains 3 (one occurrence of 3) and the factorisation of 360 contains 3 × 3 (two occurrences of 3s). Choosing two occurrences of 3s as part of LCM will contain both these subparts."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.229,
            0.534,
            0.252
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "How many 5s should the LCM contain?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.256,
            0.847,
            0.312
          ],
          "content": "The factorisation of 96 doesn’t have any 5s and the factorisation of 360 has one occurrence of 5. So, we choose one occurrence of 5 to be a part of the LCM."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.175,
            0.313,
            0.843,
            0.332
          ],
          "content": "Thus, the LCM of 96 and 360 will be 2 × 2 × 2 × 2 × 2 × 3 × 3 × 5 = 1440."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.344,
            0.848,
            0.42
          ],
          "content": "To build the LCM of two numbers, we can identify all the prime factors and find the maximum number of times each of them occur in either of the factorisations. This process can be extended to find the LCM of two or more numbers."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.431,
            0.315,
            0.454
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Figure it Out"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.459,
            0.549,
            0.529
          ],
          "content": "Find the LCM of the following numbers: (a) 30, 72 (b) 36, 54 (c) 105, 195, 65 (d) 222, 370"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.552,
            0.817,
            0.577
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "3.3 Patterns, Properties, and a Pretty Procedure!"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.586,
            0.778,
            0.607
          ],
          "content": "The HCF of 6 and 18 is 6, which is one of the two given numbers."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.621,
            0.845,
            0.661
          ],
          "content": "？ Find more such number pairs where the HCF is one of the two numbers. How can we describe such pairs of numbers?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.666,
            0.846,
            0.685
          ],
          "content": "We can see that it happens when one number is a factor of the other. This"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.686,
            0.505,
            0.819
          ],
          "content": "also means the other number will be a multiple of the first number! Such a statement describing a pattern or a property that holds in all possible cases is called a **general statement.** This process is called **generalisation.**"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.151,
            0.82,
            0.505,
            0.876
          ],
          "content": "Such a generalisation can also be described using algebra. Let us see how."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "bbox": [
            0.519,
            0.707,
            0.892,
            0.892
          ],
